": 84073, "role": "producer/director", "role_id": 31, "is_primary": true}], "also_known_as": "", "date_of_birth": "04 MAY 1932", "date_of_death": "13 OCT 2004", "genders": [{"id": 9, "name": "Male", "slug": "male"}], "languages": [{"id": 1, "name": "English", "slug": "english"}, {"id": 4, "name": "French", "slug": "french"}], "places_of_operation": [{"id": 10, "name": "United Kingdom", "slug": "united-kingdom"}], "biography": "Ivor Sydney Wood (4 May 1932 – 13 October 2004) was a prolific Anglo-French animator, director, producer and writer. He was known for his work on children's television series.Born in Leeds to an English father and a French mother, his family moved to the mountains near Lyon, France, after the Second World War, where he was educated. He studied fine art in Paris, and later worked in an advertising agency in Paris, where he met Serge Danot. Together they made the acclaimed French series Le Manège enchanté (known in English as The Magic Roundabout), with Wood as the animator.Following the success of The Magic Roundabout in the UK, Wood partnered with the London-based animation company FilmFair. Wood became both animator and director for a number of FilmFair's animated children's programmes, starting with The Herbs in 1968. During the 1970s, he animated and directed Simon in the Land of Chalk Drawings, Hattytown Tales, The Adventures of Parsley, The Wombles, and Paddington. Source: Wikidata; 01/08/2023", "biography_author": null, "date_of_biography": "2024-01-10T03:45:17.285651+11:00", "external_links": [{"id": 4209, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivor_Wood"}], "uuid": "ef39a270-8f87-44d5-a0d7-d5e20a26a99d", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "20937", "external_references": [{"id": 26617, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wikidata"}, "source_identifier": "Q3131882"}, {"id": 29050, "source": {"name": "VIAF", "slug": "viaf"}, "source_identifier": "15337929"}, {"id": 29051, "source": {"name": "LOC Auth", "slug": "loc-auth"}, "source_identifier": "n50016039"}, {"id": 29052, "source": {"name": "WorldCat", "slug": "worldcat"}, "source_identifier": "lccn-n50016039"}], "date_modified": "2024-01-10T03:45:17.285910+11:00"}, {"id": 19133, "name": "Maurice Sendak", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 759632, "title": "Alligators all around", "work_id": 75466, "role": "director", "role_id": 938, "is_primary": true}, {"id": 828812, "title": "Chicken soup with rice", "work_id": 97399, "role": "director", "role_id": 938, "is_primary": true}, {"id": 865993, "title": "One was Johnny (K)", "work_id": 80770, "role": "director", "role_id": 938, "is_primary": true}, {"id": 759647, "title": "One was Johnny", "work_id": 75471, "role": "director", "role_id": 938, "is_primary": true}, {"id": 768492, "title": "Really Rosie", "work_id": 78966, "role": "director", "role_id": 938, "is_primary": true}, {"id": 759897, "title": "Pierre", "work_id": 75590, "role": "director", "role_id": 938, "is_primary": true}], "also_known_as": "Maurice Bernard Sendak", "date_of_birth": "1928-06-10", "date_of_death": "2012-05-08", "genders": [{"id": 9, "name": "Male", "slug": "male"}], "languages": [{"id": 1, "name": "English", "slug": "english"}], "places_of_operation": [{"id": 1, "name": "United States of America", "slug": "united-states-of-america"}], "biography": "Maurice Bernard Sendak (; June 10, 1928 – May 8, 2012) was an American author and illustrator of children's books. He became most widely known for his book Where the Wild Things Are, first published in 1963. \nBorn to Polish-Jewish parents, his childhood was affected by the death of many of his family members during the Holocaust. Sendak also wrote works such as In the Night Kitchen, Outside Over There, and illustrated many works by other authors including the Little Bear books by Else Holmelund Minarik.", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2023-08-01T16:10:32.025469+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 4660, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org